# Maximum Alternating Sum of Squares

**LeetCode Problem:** [Maximum Alternating Sum of Squares](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-alternating-sum-of-squares/)

## Approach: Greedy + Sorting

### Intuition
- Take the absolute value of every element because squaring removes the sign.
- Sort the array in ascending order.
- To maximize the alternating sum, assign the largest `ceil(n/2)` elements to the positive group and the remaining elements to the negative group.
- Calculate the sum of squares of both groups and return their difference.

### C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    long long maxAlternatingSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = abs(nums[i]);
        }

        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int pivot = (n % 2 == 0) ? n / 2 : (n / 2) + 1;

        int i = n - 1, count = 0;
        long long neg_sum = 0, pos_sum = 0;

        for (i; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (count >= pivot) break;

            pos_sum += 1LL * nums[i] * nums[i];
            count++;
        }

        for (; i >= 0; i--) {
            neg_sum += 1LL * nums[i] * nums[i];
        }

        return pos_sum - neg_sum;
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** `O(n log n)` — sorting the array dominates.
- **Space Complexity:** `O(1)` auxiliary space, excluding the sorting implementation.

### Key Takeaway
Sorting allows us to place larger squared values in the positive group and smaller squared values in the negative group to maximize the alternating sum.
